What s.pos[0] refers to
if s in enemies:
    s.pos[0] -= rounded_move_by

What s.pos is

s.pos is not a Pygame thing — it’s something you (or the original author) added.

Most likely:

s.pos is a list or vector holding the enemy’s precise world position

Example:

s.pos = [x, y]


or

s.pos = pygame.math.Vector2(x, y)

What [0] means

[0] → the x-coordinate

[1] → the y-coordinate

So:

s.pos[0] -= rounded_move_by


means:

“Move the enemy’s world x-position left by the scroll amount.”

Why not just use s.rect.x?

Because:

rect.x is usually rounded to integers

pos often stores sub-pixel precision (floats)

Movement physics usually update pos

rect is then synced from pos

So this line keeps:

Physics position (pos)

Rendering position (rect)
in sync

What player_spawn_point[0] refers to
player_spawn_point[0] -= rounded_move_by

What player_spawn_point likely is

It’s almost certainly:

A list or tuple holding the world coordinates where the player respawns

Example:

player_spawn_point = [1000, 300]


or

player_spawn_point = pygame.math.Vector2(1000, 300)

What [0] means

Again:

[0] → x-coordinate

[1] → y-coordinate

So this line means:

“If the world scrolls, the player’s spawn location must scroll too.”

Otherwise:

You scroll the level

But the spawn point stays in the old world position

Respawning would put the player in the wrong place



--------------------------------------------------------------------(line 217)


# ...  height - p_height*player.size*16 + player.size*16*y + (255-c.a)*player.size*16)


Step 1: The starting point
height - p_height*player.size*16


height → the bottom of the screen in Pygame (remember y=0 at top)

p_height*player.size*16 → total height of the level in game units

Subtracting:

height - total_level_height


→ This sets the top of the level at the proper vertical position on the screen.

So the level doesn’t start off the screen

Think of it as the anchor point for the top of your level

Step 2: Add player.size*16*y
height - p_height*player.size*16 + player.size*16*y


y = row of the pixel in the PNG (0 at top, increasing downward)

Multiply by block size → how far down from the top of the level this object should be placed

So top pixel (y=0) stays at the top of the level, bottom pixel (y=p_height-1) moves downward

✅ This is exactly what you said: “whatever the y is on the level image can then be added (increased downward) 
and multiplied to be at the corresponding y coordinate on the screen.”

Step 3: Add alpha offset
+ (255 - c.a)*player.size*16


Small tweak based on the pixel’s alpha

Slightly pushes the object downward if it’s partially transparent

Useful for stacked objects, slopes, or subtle vertical placement

Step 4: Full picture
y_pos = height - p_height*player.size*16 + player.size*16*y + (255 - c.a)*player.size*16


height - p_height*player.size*16 → top of the level on the screen

+ player.size*16*y → row offset downward

+ (255 - c.a)*player.size*16 → fine adjustment

Top of level = screen anchor

Y in PNG = position in level

Alpha tweak = small adjustment

Exactly how you described it.



------------------------------------------------------------------------------(line 315)


Big picture first (what problem this solves)

Your game has hidden areas behind “back blocks”.

You use different sprite groups to control visibility order:

front_sprites → always visible

back_front_sprites → hidden until player is behind a wall

back_back_sprites → always behind everything (deep background)

Bridges are special:

They can exist inside hidden areas

Or in normal visible space

So after the level is created, you do a second pass to decide where each bridge goes.

The loop
for bridge in bridges:
    found = False


For each bridge:

found means:
👉  “Have we already decided that this bridge belongs to a special (non-default) layer?”

Once found is True, the bridge should not be added anywhere else.

Check against hidden back blocks
for bb in back_blocks:
    if bb.rect.colliderect(bridge.rect) and bb.touching and not found:


This checks:

Does the bridge overlap a back block?

bb.rect.colliderect(bridge.rect)


Is that back block a “hidden-area” block?

bb.touching == True


Recall:

touching=True → hidden unless player is behind wall

touching=False → always background

Have we already placed the bridge?

not found

If all conditions match
back_front_sprites.add(bridge)
found = True


Meaning:

“This bridge is inside a hidden area — put it in the hidden layer.”

Once found = True, no other placement should happen.

Check against water areas
for w in waters:
    if w.rect.colliderect(bridge.rect) and bb.touching and not found:
        back_back_sprites.add(bridge)
        found = True


Intent (important):

If the bridge overlaps water inside a hidden area, push it even further back

So this places the bridge:

behind water

behind hidden walls

👉 Deep background layer

⚠️ Bug note (very important):
This line:

and bb.touching


uses bb from the previous loop.

If back_blocks is empty or the loop ended differently, this could be wrong.

It probably should be:

and w.touching   # or some other water property


This works only because bb still exists from the prior loop — but it’s fragile.

Default case: visible bridge
if not found:
    front_sprites.add(bridge)


If:

The bridge didn’t overlap any hidden blocks

Didn’t overlap water in hidden areas

Then:

“This is a normal bridge — show it all the time.”

Final mental model (easy recall)
For each bridge:
    If it overlaps a hidden back block:
        Put it in hidden layer
    Else if it overlaps hidden water:
        Put it even further back
    Else:
        Make it always visible

Why this is done AFTER level creation

You can’t know a bridge’s visibility when placing it because:

It depends on overlapping objects

Those objects are created in a different pass

So you:

Create everything

Then resolve layer membership

This is good engine design.

One small improvement (optional but recommended)

You can break early once found:

if found:
    break


