1️⃣ Preparing the animation frames
ani_pics = []


This will hold every frame of the animation, precomputed.

Load the base image
fire_ball_img = get_image(image.load('images/miscSheet.png'),
                          get_cos('images/miscCo.txt')[8])
fire_ball_img = transform.scale(fire_ball_img, (64,64))


What this does:

Loads a sprite sheet (miscSheet.png)

Extracts one sprite (index 8)

Scales it to 64×64

At this point:

fire_ball_img = a single 64×64 fireball image

Create many scaled versions of that image
for i in range(359, -1, -10):
    ani_pics.append(transform.scale(fire_ball_img, i))


This is the key visual trick.

range(359, -1, -10) produces:

359, 349, 339, ..., 9


Each value of i is used as the new size

So this generates:

Very large fireball

Slightly smaller

Slightly smaller

…

Almost invisible

All stored in:

ani_pics = [Surface, Surface, Surface, ...]


You’re precomputing frames so the animation loop is fast.

⚠️ Note: This works only because Pygame interprets scale(surface, i) as (i, i)

2️⃣ The loading animation loop
def loading_animation(delay):


This function is meant to run in a separate thread while assets load.

Infinite cycling over frames
for c in itertools.cycle(ani_pics):


itertools.cycle:

Loops through ani_pics forever

Never stops unless you break

So this is your animation loop.

Exit condition
if loading_images_done:
    break


Another thread sets:

loading_images_done = True


This cleanly stops the animation when loading finishes.

3️⃣ Drawing each frame
screen.fill(BLACK)


Clears the screen each frame.

Center the current frame
tmpRect = c.get_rect()
tmpRect.center = width - randint(49,51), height - randint(49,51)


Two things happening:

get_rect() creates a rect matching the current image size

center is set near the bottom-right corner

The randint(49,51) introduces a tiny jitter, so the spinner:

Looks more alive

Doesn’t feel perfectly static

Visually, it “vibrates” slightly.

Draw and update screen
screen.blit(c, tmpRect)
display.flip()


This draws the frame and presents it immediately.

Control animation speed
time.wait(delay)


Pauses between frames.

Smaller delay → smoother / faster animation
Larger delay → slower pulsing effect

What this looks like in motion

What the player sees:

A fireball near the bottom-right

It shrinks steadily

Then jumps back to large

Repeats while loading

Slight jitter gives it life

This is essentially a pulsing shrink animation.


