for i in range(91):


This runs 91 frames of animation.

So scrolling takes about:

91 frames

at ~60 FPS → ~1.5 seconds

This is your smooth transition instead of instant teleport.

🧼 Clear screen
screen.fill(WHITE)


Erase previous frame.

🖼️ Background scrolling (parallax)
if background:
    if d == 'right':
        menu_back_rect.x -= round(2.3 * cos(radians(i)))


This moves the background a tiny amount per frame.

Important parts:

✔ Direction

If direction is 'right', background moves left:

menu_back_rect.x -= ...


That creates the illusion that camera moved right.

✔ Why cosine?
cos(radians(i))


Cosine starts near 1 and slowly eases toward 0.

So movement is:

fast at first

slows down smoothly

This is called easing — it feels more natural than constant speed.

✔ Why 2.3?
2.3 * cos(...)


This is the speed scale.
Background scrolls slowly for depth effect (parallax).


🔁 What cos(radians(i)) is doing in animation

You had something like:

menu_back_rect.x -= round(2.3 * cos(radians(i)))


and i goes from 0 → 90.

So every frame, you move the object by:

step = 2.3 × cos(angle)

📐 Step 1 — What cosine looks like

Cosine of an angle:

angle	cos(angle)
0°	1.0
30°	0.866
60°	0.5
90°	0.0

So as the angle increases from 0 → 90:

cosine starts at 1

smoothly decreases to 0

Graphically, that’s a smooth curve, not a straight line.

🚀 Step 2 — How that becomes easing

If each frame you move by:

movement = max_speed × cos(angle)


then:

At the start:
cos(0°) = 1 → full movement


➡ fast

Near the end:
cos(80°) ≈ 0.17 → tiny movement


➡ slow

At the end:
cos(90°) = 0 → stop


➡ perfectly settles

So instead of moving:

same distance every frame  → robotic


you get:

big steps → medium → tiny → stop   → natural


That’s ease-out motion.



✅ Step 1: What your code actually feeds into cosine

You wrote:

cos(radians(i))


And i goes:

0 → 90   (degrees)


After conversion:

radians(0)  = 0
radians(90) = π/2 ≈ 1.57


So cosine is seeing:

cos(0 → π/2)

✅ Step 2: What cosine DOES on that interval

Cosine values:

angle (deg)	angle (rad)	cos(angle)
0°	0	1.0
30°	0.52	0.866
60°	1.05	0.5
90°	1.57	0.0

So as the angle increases:

the cosine decreases smoothly from 1 → 0

That’s just how the cosine curve behaves on the first quadrant of the circle.

🟢 Visual intuition (unit circle)

On the unit circle:

cosine = x-coordinate

sine = y-coordinate

At:

0°

Point = (1, 0)
→ cos = 1

90°

Point = (0, 1)
→ cos = 0

So as you rotate from 0° to 90°, the x-value slides from 1 down to 0.

That x-value is exactly what cos() returns.

🔁 Why this is perfect for easing

Your movement step is:

step = speed * cos(angle)


So:

at start: cos = 1 → full speed

near end: cos ≈ 0.1 → tiny speed

end: cos = 0 → stop

You are not mapping angle to position,
you are mapping angle to velocity multiplier.

That’s the crucial idea.

⚠️ Important distinction

Angle behavior:

0 → 90   (degrees)
0 → π/2  (radians)


Cosine behavior on that interval:

1 → 0


So you’re using:

angle as a parameter to drive a curve, not as the thing being animated.

🧠 If cosine went from 0 → 1 instead…

That would be sine:

sin(0) = 0
sin(90°) = 1


So:

sin(0 → π/2) gives 0 → 1 (ease-in)

cos(0 → π/2) gives 1 → 0 (ease-out)

Same angles, different curve shape.

🔥 One-sentence mental model

Think of i as:

“How far along the animation am I?”

And cos(radians(i)) as:

“Given that progress, how fast should I be moving right now?”

Not position — speed.




🖼️ Draw background + overlay
screen.blit(menu_back, menu_back_rect)
screen.blit(alpha_screen, (0,0))


Draw menu background

Draw transparent dark overlay (probably for fade / shading)

🧍 Preview player scrolling
if player:
    if d == 'right':
        preview_player_rect.x -= round(16.58 * cos(radians(i)))


Same easing motion, but:

Much bigger movement (16.58 vs 2.3)

Player moves faster than background

This creates foreground motion, enhancing depth.

Different Y speeds:

11.06 for vertical
16.58 for horizontal


Likely tuned so player ends up perfectly aligned on next menu tile.

🎞️ Pick animation frame
current_animation = preview_animations[current_preview_player][int(ani_counter)%2]


Breakdown:

preview_animations[player] → list of frames

% 2 → toggles between frame 0 and 1

Creates walking/idle animation

So the preview character animates while sliding.



🫧 1. Bubble wobble amount
tmp = round(4 * sin(radians(ani_counter * 70)))


This computes a small vertical pulse for the bubble radius.

ani_counter → slowly increasing animation time

* 70 → makes the sine wave oscillate faster

sin() → smooth back-and-forth motion

* 4 → max ±4 pixels

round() → integer pixels for clean drawing

So tmp is in range roughly:

-4 to +4


This is used to slightly expand and shrink the bubble radius → looks like pulsing energy / wobbling bubble.

🫧 2. Draw glowing bubble behind player
Filled transparent glow
gfxdraw.filled_circle(
    screen,
    preview_player_rect.centerx,
    preview_player_rect.centery,
    196 + tmp,
    (buttons[current_preview_player].hover_color + (100,))
)


Breakdown:

Centered on player preview

Radius = 196 + tmp → pulsing size

Color = hover color of current button + alpha 100

So this is:
👉 big soft translucent glow behind the player

Bubble outline ring
draw.circle(
    screen,
    buttons[current_preview_player].button_color,
    preview_player_rect.center,
    196 + tmp,
    5
)


Same center, same pulsing radius:

Draws only the outline

Thickness = 5 pixels

So now you have:

glowing filled circle

bright ring around it

That’s your bubble effect.

🧍 3. Draw player preview facing mouse
if mx < preview_player_rect.centerx:
    screen.blit(transform.flip(current_animation, 1, 0), preview_player_rect)
else:
    screen.blit(current_animation, preview_player_rect)


(current_animation, 1, 0):
This makes the character look at your mouse:

If mouse is left of player → flip sprite horizontally

Otherwise → normal sprite

So player preview reacts to cursor position.
Nice UI polish again.

In Pygame:

pygame.transform.flip(surface, flip_x, flip_y)

✅ Parameters
transform.flip(current_animation, 1, 0)


means:

current_animation → the image (Surface) to flip

1 (or True) → flip horizontally (left ↔ right)

0 (or False) → don’t flip vertically (up ↔ down)



🎞️ 4. Advance animation timer
ani_counter += 0.04


This drives:

walk animation frame switching

bubble wobble sine wave

Small increment = smooth slow animation.

🔘 5. Scroll all buttons with the menu
for b in range(len(buttons)):


Each button is moved by the same scrolling amount as the player.

Example for right scroll:

buttons[b].pos[0] -= round(16.58 * cos(radians(i)))
buttons[b].rect.x -= round(16.58 * cos(radians(i)))


So:

visual rect moves

logical stored position moves

Why both?
Because probably:

.pos is used for logic/layout

.rect is used for drawing & collision

This keeps buttons glued to the same screen region during scroll.

Then:

screen.blit(buttons[b].image, buttons[b].rect)


Draw each button in new position.

🎮 6. Move controls panel
if d == 'right':
    controls_image_rect.x -= round(16.58 * cos(radians(i)))
...


Same scroll logic:

controls help graphic slides with the menu

So when menu scrolls, everything moves together:

player preview

buttons

controls display

title text

🏷️ 7. Move title text
if d == 'right':
    title_text_rect.x -= round(16.58 * cos(radians(i)))
...


Same idea as controls panel.

Keeps the title centered in the new menu page after scroll completes.

🧾 8. Draw background boxes and UI panels
Controls background box
gfxdraw.box(
    screen,
    (controls_image_rect.centerx - controls_back[0]/2,
     controls_image_rect.centery - controls_back[1]/2,
     controls_back[0],
     controls_back[1]),
    (GREY + (150,))
)


Draws a translucent grey rectangle behind controls image.


🧠 What gfxdraw.box expects
gfxdraw.box(surface, rect, color)


rect must be:

(x, y, width, height)


And x, y must be the top-left corner of the box.

But you don’t want the box positioned by top-left —
you want it centered behind the controls image.

So you must convert:

center position → top-left position

📦 What these variables mean
Controls image center
controls_image_rect.centerx
controls_image_rect.centery


This is the center of the controls image.

That’s where the background box should also be centered.

Background box size
controls_back[0]  # width
controls_back[1]  # height


This is how big the background box should be.

🎯 Goal

We want:

box.center == controls_image_rect.center

But gfxdraw.box needs:

box.topleft

So we must convert.

🧮 The math (this is the key idea)

To center a rectangle:

topleft_x = center_x - (width / 2)
topleft_y = center_y - (height / 2)


That’s exactly what this code is doing:

controls_image_rect.centerx - controls_back[0] / 2
controls_image_rect.centery - controls_back[1] / 2


So:

Start at center of controls image

Move left by half the box width

Move up by half the box height

Now the box is perfectly centered behind it.



Then:

screen.blit(controls_image, controls_image_rect)


So controls have a backdrop for readability.

Title background box
gfxdraw.box(
    screen,
    (title_text_rect.x - 50,
     title_text_rect.y - 50,
     title_text_rect.width + 100,
     title_text_rect.height + 100),
    GREY + (100,)
)


Big padded translucent box behind title text.

Then:

screen.blit(title_text_render, title_text_rect)


So title is readable regardless of background.

🖱️ 9. Input + frame update
event.pump()
mx, my = mouse.get_pos()
display.flip()
time.wait(3)

event.pump()

Keeps OS events flowing so window doesn’t freeze.

mouse.get_pos()

Update mouse position for:

player facing direction next frame

display.flip()

Show everything you just drew.

time.wait(3)

Tiny delay to slow animation:

~3 ms per frame

combined with 91 iterations → smooth scroll duration

🛑 10. Unlock input
let_go = False


This probably means:

“Scrolling finished, player can click again.”

Used elsewhere to block menu input during transitions.
